// Auto-generated from a 3c v5.20f cold-boot RAM dump.
//
// Sparse representation of the M: drive metadata + default
// app data files (RAMDRIVE / WLD / SPR / DAT / WRD / AGN +
// WORLD.WLD, SHEET.SPR, DATA.DBF, WORD.WRD, AGENDA.AGN). The
// captured region spans 0x80000..0x88000 of a working 3c boot;
// only the non-zero bytes are kept (1.8 KiB across 42 chunks)
// to keep the binary footprint negligible.
//
// Intended for use by 3a v3.40f / Siena v4.20f to overlay a
// coherent M: drive after the kernel writes its volume label,
// since those older kernels never lay down the subdir tree
// themselves. NOTE: experiments showed that overlaying these
// bytes does NOT in itself cure "Media is corrupt" / "Process
// exited" — the v3.40f / v4.20f kernels manage their M:-drive
// metadata in internal data structures (likely the EFSYS$M.IMG
// driver state) rather than reading from these RAM offsets.
// A real fix needs either a v3.40f / v4.20f-specific cold-boot
// shim or a driver-level intercept; this blob is kept around
// as a reference for what the kernel-managed structure SHOULD
// look like once a fix is in place. Enable injection at runtime
// via PSION_MDRIVE_INJECT=1 for diagnostic experiments.
//
// Coalesce gap = 8; offsets are relative to
// the BPB base (i.e. add the per-device M:-drive base address to
// each chunk offset before copying).
